            <ArticleTitle>Supracubital perineurioma misdiagnosed as carpal tunnel syndrome: case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>19</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Perineuriomas have been defined as tumorous lesions of the peripheral nerves which derive from perineurial cell proliferation and may be associated with abnormalities on chromosome 22.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE PRESENTATION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Three years after a painful cubital vein procaine injection, a 33 year-old man developed a median nerve lesion, initially diagnosed as carpal tunnel syndrome. Symptoms progressed despite appropriate surgery. Clinical and electrophysiological re-evaluation revealed a fusiform mass at the distal upper arm, confirmed by MRI. Immunohistochemical studies classified the tumor as a mixed perineurioma and neuroma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Perineurioma mixed with neuroma may potentially caused by the previous trauma or cytotoxic effects of procaine.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
